```text
poll 1: 10 records   positions {0=10, 1=0, 2=0}
        committed {0=10, 1=0, 2=0}
poll 2: 10 records   positions {0=12, 1=8, 2=0}
        committed {0=12, 1=8, 2=0}
poll 3: 10 records   positions {0=12, 1=9, 2=9}
        committed {0=12, 1=9, 2=9}
poll 4:  0 records   positions {0=12, 1=9, 2=9}
...
poll 10:  0 records   positions {0=12, 1=9, 2=9}
wakeup(): the loop stops and the consumer closes
```
Each poll returned at most 10 records, and the positions moved as soon as it returned. The keys put 12, 9 and 9 payments in the three partitions. Empty polls each waited the full 500 ms, which is why seven of them fill the rest of the 4 seconds. Your first line may instead be `poll 1:  0 records   positions {}`: the consumer was still joining the group and had no partitions yet. The second run writes 30 more and reads only those. It starts at the committed offsets, 12, 9 and 9 (`poll 1: 10 records   positions {0=22, 1=9, 2=9}`), and ends at 24, 18 and 18.

